            "timestamp": 1773233764,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "garybeihl",
                "email": "garybeihl@microsoft.com",
                "username": "garybeihl"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 1:\n\nPatch Set 1:\n\nThanks for the review, Mayank. You raise a good point \u2014 the spec (DSP0267 v1.3.0) defines version string length as uint8 with \"up to 255 bytes,\" so PLDM_FIRMWARE_MAX_STRING = 64 is an arbitrary static storage limit, not a spec-defined one. The bounds check here protects against overflow of the current 64-byte str_data buffer. If PLDM_FIRMWARE_MAX_STRING is later raised to UINT8_MAX (which would be the spec-correct value), the check will still be correct since it validates against the constant.\n\nRaising PLDM_FIRMWARE_MAX_STRING to 255 is a separate change that affects struct sizes across the firmware update ABI \u2014 I think that warrants its own patch with maintainer input.\n\nI'll add the additional test cases you suggested (0-length, 1-byte, and overflow at UINT8_MAX+1 boundary) and address the min bound check comments."
        },
